INT. NIGHT-SHIFT BAKERY - BACK ROOM - 4 AM

Flour dust. Steel tables. Racks of dough rising under damp cloths. A radio plays something soft and unidentifiable.

MR. TEODORICO ABARA-LINDBERG (60s, apron, gentle) kneads. His assistant, PENELOPE "PENNY" NAKASHIMA-OKOYE (20s, hairnet), oils pans. At the doorway, a STRANGER in a rain-soaked coat stands with a baguette-shaped package. Behind her, hiding behind the oven door, is a small, wide-eyed apprentice, QUINCY ADEYEMI-HALVORSEN (12, flour on his nose).

STRANGER
Good morning. Are you the baker?

TEODORICO
Everyone here is the baker.

STRANGER
I mean the baker who makes the seeded rye.

TEODORICO
Ah. That's Penny.

PENNY
That's me.

STRANGER
Then I owe you an apology.

PENNY
For what?

STRANGER
I left a one-star review.

PENNY
(not looking up)
I remember.

STRANGER
I was in a mood.

PENNY
You called it "a brick with ambitions."

STRANGER
It was a very good brick.

Teodorico chuckles into the dough.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
My name is Perpetua.

PERPETUA
(correcting)
Perpetua Chen-Oyelowo. I'm the reviewer.

PENNY
We know. You've been in the paper.

PERPETUA
Then you know I'm contractually obliged to review again.

TEODORICO
Of course.

PERPETUA
I came to taste it. Before I write.

Quincy peeks around the oven.

QUINCY
It's still rising.

PERPETUA
Then I'll wait.

TEODORICO
Penny, make her tea.

PENNY
I'm making her toast first.

She slides a loaf from the rack and slices it. Perpetua sits on an overturned crate. Quincy tiptoes to sit beside her. The radio hums. Nobody, for a while, says anything.
